exports[`renderSource on buildKnowledge's output renders a fixture source as the chat reads it (snapshot; review the diff when the rendered text changes) 1`] = `
"# [sample] Why platform work
Interview: Peter's own words, in the first person.
Roles: acme. Links: https://github.com/loriensleafs/brain.

## [sample.1] Why front-end platform work?
I like building what other engineers use.

## [sample.2] Why front-end platform work? (continued)
And I like seeing a whole team move faster.

## [sample.3] How did you help with the migration?
I helped plan it.

## Not public: decline to describe these kinds of material
Colleague names.
"
`;

exports[`buildKnowledge on the real files renders the core the chat sees (snapshot; review the diff when content changes) 1`] = `
"# Resume

[basics] Peter Kloss: Front-End Platform Engineer, Design Systems and AI Developer Tooling: Front-end platform engineer with 15+ years building what product teams build on: design systems, shared packages and monorepo tooling. At Highspot, led the organization-wide MCP server that gives AI coding agents the design system’s contract, used by ~100 people, and built the platform team’s agent workflow system. Before that, led front-end architecture for FedNow, the Federal Reserve’s instant payment service. Location: Redmond, WA. Email: pkloss@gmail.com. LinkedIn: linkedin.com/in/peter-kloss-a0613061. GitHub: github.com/loriensleafs.
[role:highspot] Highspot: Sr. Software Development Engineer, Polar UI design system team, Dec 2024 – Aug 2026, Seattle, WA.
[highlight:highspot.1] Highspot, highlight 1: Led the organization-wide effort to build the polar-ui MCP server, the design system’s contract for AI coding agents, so agent-written UI follows the design system, including complex components such as the data table. It worked alongside the Figma MCP and became a standard part of every Cursor and Claude Code setup for front-end work, used by ~100 people across all application teams, design, and product.
[highlight:highspot.2] Highspot, highlight 2: Introduced many of the team’s AI development standards: spec-driven development, shared skills and agents checked into the repo, agent context files (CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md), and rules that keep agents on Polar UI components.
[highlight:highspot.3] Highspot, highlight 3: Designed and rolled out an agent workflow system for the platform team, built in TypeScript on Bun: specialist agents and skills turn specs into dependency-mapped plans, where plans and tasks both carry dependencies, so up to 4 developers and their agents know which work is unblocked, claimed, or safe to run in parallel.
[highlight:highspot.4] Highspot, highlight 4: Used that system to build two major components: a streaming Markdown component, and a TanStack-based data table, built after an AG Grid Enterprise license was blocked and defined by 21 specs and 189 requirement documents.
[highlight:highspot.5] Highspot, highlight 5: Built accessible React and TypeScript components (WCAG, ARIA) from Figma designs and design tokens, with end-to-end tests in TestCafe and Playwright and visual regression tests.
[role:fednow] Federal Reserve Bank of Boston (FedNow): Lead Front-End Engineer (promoted from Sr. UI Front End Developer), 2021 – 2024, Boston, MA.
[highlight:fednow.1] Federal Reserve Bank of Boston (FedNow), highlight 1: Led front-end architecture across FedNow applications, aligning Product, UX, back-end, QA and security teams on one consistent, scalable approach.
[highlight:fednow.2] Federal Reserve Bank of Boston (FedNow), highlight 2: Cut initial bundle size from over 15 MB to under 1 MB across multiple applications without delaying FedNow’s launch, through code splitting and lazy loading, micro-frontends, a new build setup, fewer heavy dependencies, and design system and shared packages rebuilt as tree-shakeable ES modules. A Git plugin flagged oversized modules for further splitting.
[highlight:fednow.3] Federal Reserve Bank of Boston (FedNow), highlight 3: Built and maintained the FedNow design system and shared npm packages, used by FedNow’s internal and external applications and its internal platform tools, including several QA applications.
[highlight:fednow.4] Federal Reserve Bank of Boston (FedNow), highlight 4: Wrote front-end standards adopted by 5+ teams, which reduced code review issues, and built a testing harness that raised test coverage across the organization.
[highlight:fednow.5] Federal Reserve Bank of Boston (FedNow), highlight 5: Mentored 20+ developers one-on-one and ran weekly office hours open to every development team.
[role:pippin] Pippin Title: Senior Front-End Engineer, 2020 – 2021, Boston, MA.
[highlight:pippin.1] Pippin Title, highlight 1: Drove the migration from AngularJS to React while feature work continued.
[highlight:pippin.2] Pippin Title, highlight 2: Architected and documented a design system on Chakra UI and Styled System.
[highlight:pippin.3] Pippin Title, highlight 3: Automated generation of React GraphQL queries from the schema with GraphQL Codegen; led the Stripe payments integration.
[role:linksquares] LinkSquares: Senior Front-End Engineer, 2019 – 2020, Boston, MA.
[highlight:linksquares.1] LinkSquares, highlight 1: Built a Storybook-documented design system that let the product move to a new look incrementally.
[highlight:linksquares.2] LinkSquares, highlight 2: Moved the app to a GraphQL architecture that generated fully typed fragments, queries and mutations colocated with their React components.
[highlight:linksquares.3] LinkSquares, highlight 3: Built an OCR review widget that flagged words under 90% confidence so clients could correct them quickly.
[role:sheridan] Sheridan PubFactory: Senior Front-End Engineer, 2017 – 2019, Boston, MA.
[highlight:sheridan.1] Sheridan PubFactory, highlight 1: Built a themeable design system and dynamic templates that let publishing customers create their own themes and layouts from the UI.
[role:jack-morton] Jack Morton Worldwide: Senior Front-End Engineer, 2016 – 2017, Boston, MA.
[highlight:jack-morton.1] Jack Morton Worldwide, highlight 1: Re-architected Subway’s digital menu boards, the agency’s top-priority project: dynamically loaded modules and a custom animation library tuned to proprietary hardware, migrated without missing deliverables.
[role:hbs] Harvard Business School: Front-End Engineer, 2015 – 2016. Listed under earlier roles.
[role:six-red-marbles] Six Red Marbles: Software Engineer, 2013 – 2015. Listed under earlier roles.
[role:brigham] Brigham and Women’s Hospital: UX Engineer, 2009 – 2012. Listed under earlier roles.
[skills:ai-platform] Skills: AI platform: MCP servers (including Figma MCP), Claude Code and Cursor (skills, subagents, hooks, plugins), spec-driven development, multi-agent workflow orchestration
[skills:front-end] Skills: Front end: TypeScript, React, Lit and Web Components, accessibility (WCAG, ARIA), design tokens, TanStack Table, React Query, GraphQL (Apollo, Codegen), Chakra UI, Storybook, Figma
[skills:platform-and-tooling] Skills: Platform and tooling: Bun, Node.js, monorepos, webpack, Vite, Rollup, changesets, npm publishing, GitHub Actions, Docker, Podman
[skills:testing] Skills: Testing: Jest, TestCafe, Playwright, visual regression testing
[education] Education: University of Massachusetts Amherst

# Job-search logistics

[logistics:work-arrangement] work-arrangement: Peter is open to remote, hybrid or on-site roles in the Seattle area.
[logistics:location] location: Peter is based in Redmond, Washington, in the Seattle area, and would consider relocating for the right role.
[logistics:availability] availability: Peter is available to start immediately. If a role requires him to relocate, the start date would depend on the move.
[logistics:target-roles] target-roles: Peter is looking for Senior, Staff or Lead roles in front-end platform engineering, design systems, and AI developer tooling and AI platform work.

# Catalog

[fednow-architecture] FedNow's front-end architecture and its migration: deep-dive, roles fednow. How Peter led the design of FedNow's routing-based front-end architecture, with its shared packages, and how the whole application moved to it without delaying any features or the launch.
[fednow-bundle-size] Cutting FedNow's initial bundle from over 15 MB to under 1 MB: deep-dive, roles fednow. How Peter audited FedNow's application and led the work that cut its initial bundle from over 15 MB to under 1 MB through code splitting, a new build setup and tree-shakeable ES modules.
[fednow-design-system] The FedNow design system and shared npm packages: deep-dive, roles fednow. How Peter led the team that built the FedNow design system to 1.0, with custom accessible components on Chakra UI, and then FedNow's shared routing, query, form and build utilities packages.
[fednow-standards-mentoring] FedNow front-end standards, the testing harness, and mentoring: deep-dive, roles fednow. How Peter wrote and introduced front-end standards that 5+ teams adopted, developed a testing harness, mentored 20+ developers one-on-one and ran weekly office hours open to every development team.
[highspot-agent-workflow] The agent workflow system for Highspot's platform team: deep-dive, roles highspot. How Peter designed the team's agent workflow, wrote its agents and skills, built its tooling in TypeScript on Bun, and how the team used it to build a streaming Markdown component and a data table.
[highspot-ai-standards] The AI development standards Peter introduced at Highspot: deep-dive, roles highspot. How Peter introduced many of the team's AI development standards: spec-driven development, shared skills and agents, agent context files, and rules that keep agents on Polar UI components.
"
`;

